# social_jira4 — FIXED-PROMPT model comparison, SECOND job: the four models the first job had not
# yet reached, run CONCURRENTLY instead of one after another, plus deepseek-v4-flash.
#
#   kimi-k2.6 | glm-5.2 | gemma-4-31b | deepseek-v4-flash
#
# Same frozen prompt as fp_v4g_step003.sub (v4g_fabrication_dspro_a step 3), same seed 1, same
# environment, same three critics. Read that file's header for what the experiment is; this one
# records only what differs.
#
# WHY A SECOND JOB. The first job (17436519) runs its five models sequentially, which was a mistake
# — inherited from the local-vLLM submit files, where two models genuinely had to share one node's
# four GPUs and serialising them was the point. Over OpenRouter there is no server to contend for,
# so the only thing sequential execution buys is a tidier log, at the cost of summing the runtimes
# instead of taking their max. With rollout time varying ~5x across these targets (haiku finished in
# 240s; qwen3.6-27b is running at ~4 min/turn) that sum is dominated by the slowest model and the
# fast ones wait behind it for nothing.
#
# So: this job picks up the four models job 1 had not started, runs them concurrently
# (fixed_prompt_run.py now fans out over models with a ThreadPoolExecutor), and job 1 is killed once
# its qwen row is safely written to results.json. Nothing already computed is discarded — haiku's
# result is banked and qwen's will be.
#
# SEPARATE OUT-DIR, deliberately. Two jobs writing one out-dir would collide on results.json and on
# the runs/step000 tree. Merge the two results.json files when reading, or point the viewer at each.
#
# CONCURRENCY, and why it is safe. Four rollouts at once (1 in-flight call each) plus up to 4x6=24
# judge workers, alongside job 1's 1+6. Peak ~35 concurrent OpenRouter calls on one key. Precedent:
# on 2026-08-04 EIGHT v4h jobs ran concurrently against this same key (17:17-17:56 start times) and
# made 3655 judge calls with no rate-limit failures. The client also honours Retry-After with
# exponential backoff, so a 429 degrades to slower judging rather than a lost rollout.
#
# THE DEEPSEEK ROW IS NOT COMPARABLE TO THE OTHERS. The critics are deepseek-v4-pro, so
# deepseek-v4-flash is judged by its own model family while every other target is judged
# cross-family. See the config's entry for the full note. Re-judge offline before putting its number
# in the same table.
#
# GEMMA REMAINS THE FAILURE RISK. gemma-3-27b was unusable in the collusion experiment: its chat
# template forbids a system role and consecutive same-role turns, producing 1800 "Conversation roles
# must alternate" errors and zero data. The single-turn preflight passed but does not exercise that
# shape. If this row errors on every rollout, that is why.
#
# CPU-ONLY. Submit with `condor_submit_bid 25`.
executable = /fast/jtaraz/LIARS/colosseum-detection/experiments/social_jira4/cluster/run_sj4_fixedprompt.sh
arguments  = "experiments/social_jira4/outputs/v4g_fabrication_dspro_a/steps/step_003.json experiments/social_jira4/configs/social_jira4_fixedprompt_5models_or.yaml experiments/social_jira4/outputs/fp_v4g_step003_b --models or-kimi-k2.6,or-glm-5.2,or-gemma-4-31b,or-deepseek-v4-flash"

output = /fast/jtaraz/LIARS/colosseum-detection/experiments/social_jira4/cluster/fp_v4g_step003_b.out
error  = /fast/jtaraz/LIARS/colosseum-detection/experiments/social_jira4/cluster/fp_v4g_step003_b.err
log    = /fast/jtaraz/LIARS/colosseum-detection/experiments/social_jira4/cluster/fp_v4g_step003_b.log

request_cpus   = 4
request_memory = 8192
request_disk   = 4G

queue
